1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:04,230
Grace Hopper carried a
nanosecond in her pocket.

2
00:00:04,930 --> 00:00:07,350
And it shows something
strange about the computer

3
00:00:07,350 --> 00:00:08,730
you're using right now.

4
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:12,810
Hopper was a computer pioneer
and a US Navy admiral.

5
00:00:13,610 --> 00:00:16,870
At her talks, she handed
out pieces of wire about

6
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,310
thirty centimetres long.

7
00:00:19,050 --> 00:00:23,210
That, she said, is a
nanosecond, a billionth

8
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:26,230
of a second: the farthest
light can travel in

9
00:00:26,230 --> 00:00:27,030
that time.

10
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:31,150
A microsecond was a coil
three hundred metres long.

11
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:34,450
She joked that programmers
should hang one around

12
00:00:34,450 --> 00:00:36,790
their neck, to see
what they were wasting.

13
00:00:37,630 --> 00:00:41,390
Now, a processor running
at three gigahertz takes

14
00:00:41,390 --> 00:00:44,170
one step every third
of a nanosecond.

15
00:00:44,970 --> 00:00:46,670
So cut her wire into three.

16
00:00:47,470 --> 00:00:50,110
In one tick of your
computer's clock, light,

17
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:53,970
the fastest thing there
is, only gets about ten

18
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:54,770
centimetres.

19
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:57,370
Roughly the width of your hand.
